<h2 id="use-animations-wisely">Use animations wisely</h2>
<p>There is a whole lot that goes into giving a good talk, and how your slides look is <strong>not</strong> more important than what you talk about, how you structure your talk, and how you engage with the audience. That said, I think the slides academics use need a little more delight and tasteful design. Too many talks have slides filled with text that the audience can only read at the expense of ignoring the speaker, or overwhelm the audience with massive diagrams that are difficult to follow in the 5–30 seconds the slide is visible.</p>
<p>Use your slides as a rhetorical tool: use them to accentuate and illustrate your point while the audience listens to <em>you</em>. Use animations where it makes sense to keep the audience’s focus where your talking points land. Always make sure it illuminates and never distracts.</p>
